      "text": "Honestly, **no, these support terms are not good for a production system.** Here's why:\n\n- **9x5 business hours only** — This means if your system goes down at 6pm on a Friday, on a weekend, or during a holiday, you won't get support until the next business morning. Most production systems run 24/7 and need to handle incidents outside normal office hours.\n\n- **No SLA on the base tier** — An SLA (Service Level Agreement) guarantees response and resolution times, and typically includes financial penalties if the vendor misses them. Without one, there's no contractual obligation for the vendor to respond promptly or at all when something breaks.\n\nFor a production system, you'd generally want **24/7 support with an SLA** guaranteeing things like:\n- 1-hour response time for critical outages\n- Defined resolution timeframes\n- Credits or penalties for missing those targets\n\nThe quoted price of $4,200/month is also worth scrutinizing given the limited support, but that depends on what your workload actually requires. If this is a non-critical dev or staging environment, the terms might be acceptable. For anything that serves real users, you'd want to negotiate significantly better terms or look at a different vendor.",
